“一眼丁真” is a Chinese internet meme. 丁真 is a person from a poor rural area in China. He has little formal education. Because of his looks, a local government picked him out during a TV shoot of villagers herding sheep, made him a local publicity figure and government staffer, and later even sent him to the UN to speak about living with animals. Overnight he reached a height that most ordinary Chinese people never reach in a lifetime.

That infuriated young people who had studied for years and were still grinding in the workplace. The government keeps saying education and effort changes your fate — but 丁真’s fate changed because of his face and luck. So the internet glued 一(one/first)眼(eye) onto his name and made the word 一眼丁真, meaning “you can tell it’s real at a glance,” as a jab at official “study hard and you’ll make it” propaganda. People don’t hate Ding Zhen himself. They use him to vent about life and about some government unfairness.

(In Slay the Spire 1 there is a well-known mod called the Dingzhen mod.)

In Chinese, “Snecko” is translated as 异蛇 (“strange snake”). 异 and 一 are pronounced the same; Eye is 眼. So some STS meme-makers turn “Snecko’s Eye” into 异眼. 大费物 literally means “card costs a lot of energy” — Snecko’s Eye often rolls cards to 3 energy — and it is also a pun on “废物” (useless / a waste).

I think this really is a hard part to translate. So I think we can replace it with some Western memes about snakes, Truth, or calling out fakes.